[{:db/id "q-350322/field-1/9c6bfb4db8d042ca3b93589c844ac1c789909e0099f428d8083546a00b4d8cf7" :answer/id #uuid "db48381e-4024-5a3d-bf0a-179de7031f28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350322/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "cbc409eb-88a2-5399-a032-a13fee29e012" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-350322/field-1/9c6bfb4db8d042ca3b93589c844ac1c789909e0099f428d8083546a00b4d8cf7" :answer-field/choices ["q-350322/field-1/9c6bfb4db8d042ca3b93589c844ac1c789909e0099f428d8083546a00b4d8cf7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-350322" :question/id #uuid "16f19f36-8376-5419-9c05-595c1dfe5d3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-350322" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}\\frac{18}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =18{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =18{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-3})]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-6(0-\\frac{1}{27}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-350322/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-350322"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-152777"] :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{x}^{-9/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{7}{x}^{-7/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{7}}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{a}^{7}}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{7}}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-5962"] :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{\\theta }^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{\\theta }^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{3}{{\\theta }^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}3{\\theta }^{-3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(-\\frac{3}{2}{\\theta }^{-2})]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-152762/selection/3be4a0b33d8ae752fcff4e19cc6abeec9ea0ac2c8234b469f7c7959c2008fb10" :answer/id #uuid "fc0076a4-053c-5d0e-a5de-f00509b76469" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152762/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "50b56624-6b25-5280-bae4-4c5cab999c6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152762/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" :answer/id #uuid "7bf0e9a5-84b0-5ab4-8c5b-11285ec9c003" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152762/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "0a330799-bcaa-52e3-96f3-e8a04f4b2567" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152762/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "1540e31f-8803-5442-bb93-2f0aac2a50e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152762/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1090056a-5ddf-55ac-a3bc-d49ea2dde0ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-152762/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-152762/selection/3be4a0b33d8ae752fcff4e19cc6abeec9ea0ac2c8234b469f7c7959c2008fb10" "q-152762/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-152762/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" "q-152762/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-152762/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-152762" :question/id #uuid "729d01e4-2732-59fe-897c-1e548a2a6adf" :question/math-academy-id "q-152762" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{8}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{8}^{a}{z}^{-4/3}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-3{z}^{-1/3}]}_{8}^{a} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{z}}]}_{8}^{a} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{a}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{8}}) \\\\  & =-6(0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-152762/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-152762"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-152755/selection/3089f9a34f7a805d8b2b2d5d51b10dc7aea027e8ec8fd8d06ad11cecf2ba9740" :answer/id #uuid "6c6ad261-340f-5255-8601-541f4a965140" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152755/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" :answer/id #uuid "ead9cc6a-852d-58da-b82c-d521e12e0ba8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152755/selection/3be4a0b33d8ae752fcff4e19cc6abeec9ea0ac2c8234b469f7c7959c2008fb10" :answer/id #uuid "d6aa94af-ccc0-55da-a76e-b1438e6b6211" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152755/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer/id #uuid "95ab47d6-8d26-5b4f-9cde-a469ce9d4998" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152755/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "72dd7196-dbba-5529-bd6b-a1992d7a19a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152755/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cb009ad7-cd50-5611-87fb-b939b64a88d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-152755/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" :answer-field/choices ["q-152755/selection/3089f9a34f7a805d8b2b2d5d51b10dc7aea027e8ec8fd8d06ad11cecf2ba9740" "q-152755/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" "q-152755/selection/3be4a0b33d8ae752fcff4e19cc6abeec9ea0ac2c8234b469f7c7959c2008fb10" "q-152755/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" "q-152755/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-152755" :question/id #uuid "9f5ed776-3c7e-56db-a4c7-558bcb49c8b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-152755" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{\\theta }^{6}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{\\theta }^{6}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{2}{{\\theta }^{6}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}2{\\theta }^{-6}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(-\\frac{2}{5}{\\theta }^{-5})]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{5}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{5}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-152755/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-152755"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-118619"] :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=1+{x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=1+{x}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[-{u}^{-1}]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{a}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-152045/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "48464647-401f-59d5-8410-584cccf2c1e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152045/selection/9e11fa4df3725c69efaf2fb0a82d90e47a4519d002f721e23606c3d6290fb02c" :answer/id #uuid "ba70fdfb-73ba-540b-be2c-b03b091657d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152045/selection/3be4a0b33d8ae752fcff4e19cc6abeec9ea0ac2c8234b469f7c7959c2008fb10" :answer/id #uuid "8fe068ee-68af-5b09-b42f-d5a531978ae9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152045/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer/id #uuid "0dbeda41-874c-5363-9d23-fdfa58537673" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152045/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "cedd8645-a6a6-5b33-8ad1-d5aceb3ee75d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152045/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7a6755f4-b0c3-5433-967c-5dc8a744596a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-152045/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer-field/choices ["q-152045/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" "q-152045/selection/9e11fa4df3725c69efaf2fb0a82d90e47a4519d002f721e23606c3d6290fb02c" "q-152045/selection/3be4a0b33d8ae752fcff4e19cc6abeec9ea0ac2c8234b469f7c7959c2008fb10" "q-152045/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" "q-152045/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-152045" :question/id #uuid "84f2c245-aa22-51b2-9700-5a173726767d" :question/math-academy-id "q-152045" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(2x+4)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=2x+4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2x+4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(2x+4)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}(\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(2x+4)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{4}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-152045/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-152045"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-118618/selection/3be4a0b33d8ae752fcff4e19cc6abeec9ea0ac2c8234b469f7c7959c2008fb10" :answer/id #uuid "d7d6af93-db58-5dd4-b826-02c8d57a4b82" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118618/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "e2f7aed7-bd56-551c-8d85-296e796fdd4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118618/selection/e3b46c3e63ee8ef5a071cd8ef5f7250114c0c6983477af061861d82a06039d46" :answer/id #uuid "de5cb732-48aa-54ea-a8d4-875b4b95ab17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118618/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "183ec2b4-cb81-5e3a-8989-c0408a8fde0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118618/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "9c00069b-30cc-5963-8f54-ded2e7bedd4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118618/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9dd1ea14-5b0b-5a97-ae05-f2c71ec4dd20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-118618/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-118618/selection/3be4a0b33d8ae752fcff4e19cc6abeec9ea0ac2c8234b469f7c7959c2008fb10" "q-118618/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-118618/selection/e3b46c3e63ee8ef5a071cd8ef5f7250114c0c6983477af061861d82a06039d46" "q-118618/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-118618/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-118618" :question/id #uuid "de7d243d-3bd6-5fa9-8a5f-d044c01534a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-118618" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{2}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{2}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-118618/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-118618"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-350534/field-1/2edf353dc47d221855e5e7ee4be942392390ff01766e089c29dc368c2051b12d" :answer/id #uuid "c61687b0-9008-5ef3-a7d4-e1635208a08b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350534/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a4fe2876-2127-51de-8836-dd27c832933e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-350534/field-1/2edf353dc47d221855e5e7ee4be942392390ff01766e089c29dc368c2051b12d" :answer-field/choices ["q-350534/field-1/2edf353dc47d221855e5e7ee4be942392390ff01766e089c29dc368c2051b12d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-350534" :question/id #uuid "ffe81441-8aee-5b69-8444-b663eb3c8065" :question/math-academy-id "q-350534" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{4}+2)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{4}+2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}+2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{4}+2)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{4}+2)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-1/2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{1/2}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{1/2}}] \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-350534/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-350534"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-350532/field-1/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer/id #uuid "c2401ff7-53d7-5fc9-93dc-af89ac1a8b92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350532/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "96c69c26-abf4-5aec-b4bf-24a816db0a50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-350532/field-1/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer-field/choices ["q-350532/field-1/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-350532" :question/id #uuid "8660f5f1-f7ea-5da0-ac60-28e25b93ef09" :question/math-academy-id "q-350532" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(5\\theta +1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=5\\theta +1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $\\theta \\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=5\\theta +1$ is as follows:\n\n| $\\theta $ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(5\\theta +1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(5\\theta +1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{5}{u}^{-2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-350532/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-350532"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-350546/field-1/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer/id #uuid "a78432dd-fefe-5420-a212-8cf893e9ddf4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350546/field-1/a7643e7c9a6f47b7e23102a9f593aee2dc956fa29938bf5d470303f0587bc3b1" :answer/id #uuid "5404ad5f-58e9-533b-9474-d96ddb48ece6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350546/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7c3e5c2b-3a58-5ee8-8d1d-9b386e6cb07c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-350546/field-1/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer-field/choices ["q-350546/field-1/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" "q-350546/field-1/a7643e7c9a6f47b7e23102a9f593aee2dc956fa29938bf5d470303f0587bc3b1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-350546" :question/id #uuid "d17f08ab-cd4a-5a3a-8655-e5472408f6cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-350546" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{({x}^{3}-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}-4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}-4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-\\infty $ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-12$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{({x}^{3}-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-12}\\frac{3}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-12}\\frac{3}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-12}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-12} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-12} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-12)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{12}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-350546/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-350546"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-152077/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "c8aadcbf-ce87-5828-a6ff-13025839c032" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152077/selection/3be4a0b33d8ae752fcff4e19cc6abeec9ea0ac2c8234b469f7c7959c2008fb10" :answer/id #uuid "375a6cdc-fe86-5d4b-ba4c-116086885fb4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152077/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "9b08d82f-4118-5c78-a99c-1330faf97e9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152077/selection/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" :answer/id #uuid "b420edb3-80f8-5616-8f8f-1da7b7d3717a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152077/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "2f9b8efe-f13b-51ad-87ac-5e2155b8f9a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152077/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3212ca7a-0741-5c58-9767-3754939f6f58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-152077/selection/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" :answer-field/choices ["q-152077/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" "q-152077/selection/3be4a0b33d8ae752fcff4e19cc6abeec9ea0ac2c8234b469f7c7959c2008fb10" "q-152077/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-152077/selection/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" "q-152077/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-152077" :question/id #uuid "0f62763c-b254-5d25-9374-20539800a69a" :question/math-academy-id "q-152077" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{6}{(1+2x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=1+2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=1+2x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6}{(1+2x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{6}{{u}^{3}}(\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{6}{(1+2x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-2}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-152077/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-152077"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-350542/field-1/21bc1f5ac2b6db8ca11973d9b85c1eeb6af2cd920b556e0cf3d98ab9a728286a" :answer/id #uuid "417ab5fa-7d06-5fbe-a44a-d34b28a1ba14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350542/field-1/c43b27454cd69f1b6f4b793e211f4f4f6e40314b87f5b1c35a69172a60fcd818" :answer/id #uuid "f595509e-0c53-5e8d-a745-b731185df427" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350542/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6087e5e-901a-59e8-9916-5f25507a9f3d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-350542/field-1/21bc1f5ac2b6db8ca11973d9b85c1eeb6af2cd920b556e0cf3d98ab9a728286a" :answer-field/choices ["q-350542/field-1/21bc1f5ac2b6db8ca11973d9b85c1eeb6af2cd920b556e0cf3d98ab9a728286a" "q-350542/field-1/c43b27454cd69f1b6f4b793e211f4f4f6e40314b87f5b1c35a69172a60fcd818"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-350542" :question/id #uuid "823ca2d9-cebd-5456-8ab8-8f846682425f" :question/math-academy-id "q-350542" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{10}{(4t+3)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=4t+3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $t\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=4t+3$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{10}{(4t+3)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{5}{2{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{5}{2{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot (-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-350542/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-350542"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-20884"] :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{(7x+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=7x+3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=7x+3$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-4$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{(7x+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{7{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{(7x+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{7{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-4}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-2}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-4)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{16}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{16}-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{224}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-100495"] :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}-1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}-1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-2$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-2)^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{-8}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-101735/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "71522e6f-7b00-5d83-830e-5e5de40bdf73" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101735/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "63261215-1e5f-5294-8542-0b1c08bb9fef" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101735/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "e46d703b-f925-5221-bda1-c2baf909091a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101735/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "092eebfb-3ba9-5e64-96e1-5946b9895a40" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101735/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "cbaec9cb-c599-5ba8-ace7-af27043df259" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101735/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f1fe9e7b-b0b1-5b9c-b4be-e4a5d4296639" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-101735/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-101735/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-101735/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-101735/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-101735/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-101735/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-101735" :question/id #uuid "7047774a-5515-50b6-ae41-3214d72cda4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-101735" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z$\n- ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2(-2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{z+3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a+3}+\\frac{1}{0+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡a-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals III is divergent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-101735/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-101735"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-20700/selection/7ea009d38067b85c260cd71ce15cce585e1f931f7adf440bc470127b22bd36c2" :answer/id #uuid "130727f1-6952-5580-8169-6740133df6cc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-20700/selection/286a23b4f88ab5c5f58674d94dd3996a24158cd522f456ca4f7fa96ef4dc831e" :answer/id #uuid "1ab61766-35a3-576f-bec9-7f7a08b07f43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-20700/selection/fab65d153717b238106f94aa0e1d3b2b40ce76149923d29790de708a5cffc07d" :answer/id #uuid "e51941fe-fc63-582c-a1a4-3b35f7369225" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-20700/selection/7a1faa903ae46b0a3e4acf78c14a9880b73ef464bcd4b78c3e7a703c6538af45" :answer/id #uuid "338cfd3a-108b-5e39-b1b1-0a6286be498e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-20700/selection/15aff851ab2807102904f6e00e765d26d734a91dd538d10b770cfeb2fdeede38" :answer/id #uuid "aee64568-801b-5084-87cf-de1562f008f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-20700/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "be1759d6-e74b-5313-92ad-920a6610c440" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-20700/selection/7ea009d38067b85c260cd71ce15cce585e1f931f7adf440bc470127b22bd36c2" :answer-field/choices ["q-20700/selection/7ea009d38067b85c260cd71ce15cce585e1f931f7adf440bc470127b22bd36c2" "q-20700/selection/286a23b4f88ab5c5f58674d94dd3996a24158cd522f456ca4f7fa96ef4dc831e" "q-20700/selection/fab65d153717b238106f94aa0e1d3b2b40ce76149923d29790de708a5cffc07d" "q-20700/selection/7a1faa903ae46b0a3e4acf78c14a9880b73ef464bcd4b78c3e7a703c6538af45" "q-20700/selection/15aff851ab2807102904f6e00e765d26d734a91dd538d10b770cfeb2fdeede38"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-20700" :question/id #uuid "fcfebbdd-eff9-56c3-a6d3-b799bf7b081e" :question/math-academy-id "q-20700" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 2 & \\infty  \\\\ u & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{a}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{ln}⁡a-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)] \\\\  & =\\infty -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-20700/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-20700"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-101738/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "efc349af-feca-5ce6-bde4-0514bcf35c8e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101738/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "033cdbf6-5063-581f-b461-3b0faf064cb6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101738/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "f4352038-3641-5325-bff8-6be663fc47e8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101738/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "cd99f019-41a9-58b2-a52d-6c041413a640" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101738/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "5a921231-d28d-5536-b5ed-4286d39cbb6f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101738/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6439cc0a-0930-5c97-8ab6-605bd4ba7720" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-101738/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-101738/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-101738/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-101738/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-101738/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-101738/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-101738" :question/id #uuid "881ddc28-aa2d-5caa-acf7-65aca1980081" :question/math-academy-id "q-101738" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t$\n- ${\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{u-1}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{a-1}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(0-{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{a}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[4\\sqrt{x-2}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt{a-2}-\\sqrt{3-2}) \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt{a-2}-1) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\nIn conclusion, only integrals II and III are divergent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-101738/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-101738"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-100643/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "cef778bd-e80a-5d89-b7c3-84b1af025f4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100643/selection/ae36f031db2e50be55521c1089ba53f85114c90dc4cadf7806953380ac541039" :answer/id #uuid "983bd647-d383-5fa7-8e41-7ad8f6bc2982" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100643/selection/3a619fab2616dab0463f9c76cb72cd9f101e45e62111356a5b0b111e062a8dcf" :answer/id #uuid "8cfb9772-703c-5029-8934-44d2218e7823" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100643/selection/22808498a9dab5f502b3c6caf3659d3c09bea7d00272f28e595a91887b73521c" :answer/id #uuid "225791c4-85a1-5fb9-b8b0-1b40dbdce621" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100643/selection/7ea009d38067b85c260cd71ce15cce585e1f931f7adf440bc470127b22bd36c2" :answer/id #uuid "a397f02c-092f-53f4-858d-8da109ab5b1c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100643/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "17af4f3e-9c6d-53fa-a9d3-5dfe65dcda8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-100643/selection/7ea009d38067b85c260cd71ce15cce585e1f931f7adf440bc470127b22bd36c2" :answer-field/choices ["q-100643/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" "q-100643/selection/ae36f031db2e50be55521c1089ba53f85114c90dc4cadf7806953380ac541039" "q-100643/selection/3a619fab2616dab0463f9c76cb72cd9f101e45e62111356a5b0b111e062a8dcf" "q-100643/selection/22808498a9dab5f502b3c6caf3659d3c09bea7d00272f28e595a91887b73521c" "q-100643/selection/7ea009d38067b85c260cd71ce15cce585e1f931f7adf440bc470127b22bd36c2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-100643" :question/id #uuid "40d5ff9f-2d78-5585-b446-0d19f9f03908" :question/math-academy-id "q-100643" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-3t}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-3t}} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-3t}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{2-3t}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\sqrt{2+3}-\\sqrt{2-3a}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{2-3a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-100643/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-100643"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-6032"] :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- ${\\int }_{10}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{10}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{10}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{x-1}]}_{10}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-1}-2\\sqrt{10-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-1}-6) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|) \\\\  & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a}+\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals I and II are divergent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-511"] :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_2^\\infty \\dfrac { 8}{t^5}  \\,\\textrm{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{8}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{t}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{-4}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{4}}) \\\\  & =-2(0-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-10717"] :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{u^2} \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{a}-\\frac{1}{1}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-512"] :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that, this time, it's the *lower* limit of integration that's unbounded.  \n\nFirst, we substitute $u=x-4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty$ when $x\\to -\\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x-4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-4$ | $-\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\\int_{-\\infty}^{-4} \\dfrac{1}{u^2}  \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-4)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-513"] :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x} }\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{-2} \\frac{1}{(1+x)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\,\\textrm{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\n- For the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[2{x}^{1/2}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[\\sqrt{x}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\sqrt{b}-1] \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\n- For the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{1+x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[-\\frac{1}{1-2}+\\frac{1}{1+a}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{1+a}+1] \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\n- For the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2({1}^{2})}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\nIn conclusion, only integral I is divergent." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
